<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">L'Anse aux Meadows</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1000</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">L'Anse aux Meadows is an archaeological site at the northern tip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newfoundland">Newfoundland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, the only confirmed Norse settlement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>. Discovered in 1960 by Helge Ingstad and excavated with Anne Stine Ingstad, it contained the remains of turf buildings occupied briefly around 1000; tree-ring evidence points to activity in 1021. It is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lanse-aux-meadows.jpg" alt="L'Anse aux Meadows" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dylan Kereluk from White Rock, Canada · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Authentic_Viking_recreation.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The site lies near St. Anthony on the northernmost tip of the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newfoundland">Newfoundland</a>. The name is a French-English one that can be translated as Grassland Bay, though how the village acquired it is debated. It may be a corruption of the French L'Anse aux Méduses, Jellyfish Cove, or derive from L'Anse à la Médée, Medea Cove, as an 1862 French naval chart marks it. Five Indigenous groups occupied the place before the Norse, the oldest about 6,000 years ago, but none at the same time as the Norse. The most prominent were the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorset">Dorset</a> people, who were there about 300 years before them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Carbon dating gives the Norse settlement a range of 990 to 1050 CE, with a mean of 1014, and a 2021 study of three tree-ring samples, using the atmospheric carbon-14 anomaly of 993, pinpointed 1021 as a date of Norse activity. The remains of eight buildings, of sod over a wooden frame, were found, with over 800 Norse objects. 6 metres and had several rooms.</p><p>Others were workshops: an iron smithy with a forge and slag, a carpentry workshop and a boat repair area with worn rivets. A spindle and a needle suggest that women as well as men lived there, and butternuts, which do not grow naturally north of New Brunswick, show that the Norse travelled farther south.</p><p>How long the site was used is uncertain. John Steinberg suggests occupation at least sporadically for perhaps 20 years, while a 2019 study allows activity for as long as a century. Eleanor Barraclough suggests a temporary boat repair base rather than a permanent settlement, noting that there are no burials, farming remains or animal pens, which suggests an orderly abandonment. The dwellings could hold 30 to 160 people, far fewer than the roughly 2,500 then living in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenland">Greenland</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>In 1960 George Decker, a resident of the hamlet, led the Norwegian explorer Helge Ingstad to grassy mounds the locals called the old Indian camp. Helge Ingstad and the archaeologist Anne Stine Ingstad led excavations from 1961 to 1968 and identified a Norse settlement by comparison with sites in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenland">Greenland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iceland">Iceland</a> from around 1000 CE. Parks <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> later ordered further excavations, under Bengt Schonbach in 1973 to 1975 and Birgitta Wallace in 1976. The site became a National Historic Site of Canada in November 1968 and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> in 1978.</p><p>It is the only confirmed Norse site in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenland">Greenland</a>. It has been claimed to be either Leifsbudir or Hóp, settlements of Vinland named in the Saga of the Greenlanders and the Saga of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erik-the-red">Erik the Red</a>, but Stuart C. Brown found Ingstad's attempt to show it was Leifsbudir wholly unconvincing.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “L'Anse aux Meadows” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Norwegians settled the Faroe Islands, Iceland, Shetland, Orkney, the Hebrides and Man, raided and settled in Ireland and northern Britain, and from Iceland reached Greenland and, around 1000, Newfoundland, where the Norse site at <b>L'Anse aux Meadows</b> has been excavated.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-norway">Kingdom of Norway · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Norse speakers also settled in Normandy, whose name means “land of the Northmen”, in the Faroe Islands, in Iceland, and in Greenland, where Erik the Red founded settlements around 985; around 1000 Norse sailors reached North America, at <b>L’Anse aux Meadows</b> in Newfoundland.</blockquote><a href="/language/old-norse">Old Norse · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Wood cut with metal blades at <b>L’Anse aux Meadows</b> in Newfoundland has been dated to the year 1021 by a spike in radiocarbon that a solar storm of 993 left in tree rings: the earliest exact year known for Europeans in the Americas.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vikings">Vikings · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
